# ── server ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NODE_ENV=development
SERVER_HOST=0.0.0.0
SERVER_PORT=4000
LOG_LEVEL=debug

# Per-IP request throttling (express-rate-limit). Defaults: on outside
# NODE_ENV=test. RATE_LIMIT_MAX is the general per-window cap; auth/session
# endpoints use the tighter RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_MAX. Health + SSE are exempt.
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED left commented — `bool()` in config/env.ts only applies
# its NODE_ENV-derived default (on outside test) when the var is ABSENT from
# process.env; writing `=true` here would win over that default even under
# NODE_ENV=test, since dotenv loads .env unconditionally (only .env.local is
# skipped in test mode). CI's `cp example.env .env` step turned that into a
# real bug: auth-route integration tests (e.g. apple-sign-in) tripped the
# real 20-req/60s authLimiter and got 429s instead of the responses they
# asserted. Uncomment only to force it on in a non-test run.
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000
RATE_LIMIT_MAX=300
RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_MAX=20

# Uploaded files live under DATA_DIR; logs under LOGS_DIR. The database
# itself is Postgres now (see DATABASE_URL below) and no longer lives in
# DATA_DIR.
DATA_DIR=./data
LOGS_DIR=./logs

# Postgres connection (libpq URL). Same VM as the Node server in the
# default deploy — see docs/ops/postgres.md for the one-time
# `createuser` / `createdb` setup. Add `?sslmode=require` (or stricter)
# when pointing at a managed service.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://chord@127.0.0.1:5432/chord

# Unlocks POST /api/v1/demo/reset + the "Reset demo" footer in the mobile
# app. Off by default — only turn on for live demo rigs.
DEMO_MODE=0

# ── scheduling & housekeeping ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Proactive scheduler tick cadence (ms). PROACTIVE_RATE_MINUTES was removed
# 2026-10-13 (it only ever blocked task reminders); delete it from your .env.
PROACTIVE_TICK_MS=15000
# Auto-archive threshold for DONE tasks (minutes; default 7 days). Open
# tasks are never auto-archived by age.
ARCHIVE_DONE_MINUTES=10080
# ADR 0019 — how many `other`-bucketed entities must share a pending
# folder name before the catchup classifier promotes a new folder.
CATCHUP_PROMOTION_THRESHOLD=4

# ── push notifications (Expo) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Gates device push notifications end-to-end (proactive nudges + new
# messages, fanned out to registered devices via Expo's push service).
# Off by default so tests/CI/local dev never reach Expo. Set to 1 on the
# deployed server once a TestFlight/production build is registering tokens.
PUSH_ENABLED=0
# Optional Expo access token — only needed if the Expo project enforces
# enhanced push security. Leave blank otherwise.
EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ── auth (ADR 0014) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 'google' = real users, Google OAuth required to sign in. REQUIRED for
#            the Telegram connector flow (app-first pairing): the bot
#            only recognises users who have a real Chord account, and
#            those only exist under AUTH_MODE=google.
# 'demo'   = legacy username-only POST /api/v1/session (tests, CI,
#            local mock). The bot in this mode treats all DM senders as
#            unpaired strangers since there's no Google identity to
#            link against. Never deploy with AUTH_MODE=demo.
AUTH_MODE=demo

# 32-byte key, base64-encoded. Encrypts Google refresh tokens at rest.
# Generate with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# Required when AUTH_MODE=google. Rotating it invalidates all stored
# refresh tokens — every user has to re-grant Google access.
MASTER_KEY=

# Google OAuth (ADR 0014 + 0015). Create credentials at
# https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials → OAuth client. You
# need *two* client ids registered against the same project:
#   1. Web — used by the SERVER to exchange the auth code for tokens, and
#      by the web AND Android builds at sign-in time (ADR 0081).
#   2. iOS  — used by the iOS Expo build at sign-in time.
# There is deliberately no Android client: Google dropped custom-scheme
# redirects for that type, so Android rides the web client instead.
# Each registered client must list its redirect URI in the Google console.
# The server never needs to know the mobile redirect URIs — the client
# tells it which one it used in the code-exchange request, and the server
# forwards that verbatim to Google.

# ── server-side (web client used for code exchange) ───────────────────
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Native iOS OAuth client id (public — no secret). The native app redirects to
# this client's reversed-client-id scheme, which web clients reject. Refresh
# tokens are bound to their issuing client, so the server needs this id to
# refresh tokens granted from the iOS build. Same value as
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_IOS below.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_IOS=
# The redirect URI comes from the client on every sign-in request; the
# server keeps no default. Web OAuth clients in the Google Cloud Console
# only accept HTTPS or http://localhost redirects — custom schemes
# (chord://) belong on the iOS client type, which derives its redirect
# from the bundle id automatically.

# ── mobile-side (EXPO_PUBLIC_* — read at bundle time) ─────────────────
# Two clients cover three platforms (ADR 0041 + 0081): iOS has its own
# public client; web AND Android both use the web client. Leaving a value
# empty disables sign-in on the platforms that need it.
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_IOS=
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_WEB=
# Redirect URIs the mobile build will use. Both must be registered on the
# Web OAuth client's Authorized Redirect URIs list (HTTPS or
# http://localhost only — the console rejects custom schemes there):
#   <EXPO_PUBLIC_WEB_BASE>/oauthredirect   the web flow
#   <EXPO_PUBLIC_WEB_BASE>/nativeauth      the Android flow, caught by the
#                                          App Link (ADR 0081)
# iOS needs neither: its redirect is the reversed-client-id scheme, which
# the iOS client type derives from the bundle id automatically. The app
# builds these from EXPO_PUBLIC_WEB_BASE; there is no redirect setting.

# Telegram bot handle the landing page tells users to search for /
# add to their group. Per-environment: prod = @gruntworkbot, dev =
# whatever test bot you're pointing TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN at. If unset,
# the landing copy falls back to the generic phrase "the Chord
# Telegram bot" — set this in any environment that ships the web app.
EXPO_PUBLIC_TELEGRAM_BOT_HANDLE=

# Base URL of the web app, used to build shareable group-invite links
# (`<base>/join/<CODE>`). On web the live origin is used automatically, so
# this only matters for links generated inside native builds. Must be the
# same host the native deep-link is verified against (ADR 0048): the apex
# https://chordapp.ai (no trailing slash, no subdomain).
EXPO_PUBLIC_WEB_BASE=https://chordapp.ai

# Name of the Google Calendar Chord writes events to. On first push,
# Chord looks for an existing calendar with this name (owner/writer
# access only) and binds to it. Falls back to creating a new one. Pick
# a name the user already maintains — "Family" by default so events
# land on the shared family calendar rather than littering their
# primary.
GCAL_TARGET_CALENDAR_NAME=Family

# ── In-app Gmail reader (ADR 0060/0061/0062) ──────────────────────────
# Master gate. gmail.modify is a RESTRICTED Google scope needing OAuth app
# verification — leave OFF until verified. Off hides the Connect Gmail UI
# and 404s every /me/email/* route.
GMAIL_ENABLED=0
# real | mock. 'mock' serves fixture messages so the reader/sync/reply flow
# runs with no Google account (tests, mock-LLM demos).
GMAIL_PROVIDER=real
# Bounded initial-import window, days back from now.
GMAIL_BACKFILL_DAYS=90
# Opportunistic sync throttle, minutes.
GMAIL_SYNC_THROTTLE_MINUTES=5
# Email classifier (ADR 0061 / 0066). '' disables; 'mock' = deterministic
# fixture tiles; 'llm' = in-repo LLM classifier (uses getLlmForUser / BYOK).
EMAIL_CLASSIFIER=
# Shared secret for Mode-B POST /me/email/classification without a user
# session. Send as header x-chord-classifier-token. Empty = session-only.
EMAIL_CLASSIFIER_SERVICE_TOKEN=
# Max threads classified per LLM pass (cost control).
EMAIL_CLASSIFIER_BATCH_SIZE=40
# Max category path depth (root counts as 1).
EMAIL_CLASSIFIER_MAX_DEPTH=3
# Email magics (ADR 0063): after classify, run an LLM extractor over new
# classified threads to stage calendar-event / list-item proposals. Off by
# default (one LLM call per new thread; dedup'd per thread).
EMAIL_MAGICS_ENABLED=0
# Email attachments → knowledge base (ADR 0071): after each sync, import Gmail
# attachments into the user's personal group so extraction / FTS / bot search
# see them. On by default — only messages that carry attachments cost anything.
EMAIL_ATTACHMENTS_ENABLED=1
# Max messages scanned for attachments per sync tick (backfill drains gradually).
EMAIL_ATTACHMENTS_BATCH_SIZE=10

# ── LLM ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# openai | gemini | anthropic | mock
LLM_PROVIDER=openai

# OpenAI-compatible (also covers Ollama + vLLM — just change the base URL)
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5.4-mini

# Gemini
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash

# Anthropic (Claude). Users can also bring their own key per-account in
# Settings; this section is only the deploy-wide default.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-6
# Optional: override for an Anthropic-compatible gateway. Blank = SDK default.
ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=

# ── billing + tier-based model routing (ADR 0063/0194/0195/0196) ──────
# OFF by default: the turn path is unchanged (BYOK key if present, else the
# premium system model, never debited). Turn ON to gate non-BYOK users on
# this allowance — premium model while there's balance left, then a graceful
# fall to the cheap OSS model below (never blocked). ADR 0196: "balance" is
# COMPUTED, not stored — MONTHLY_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE minus everything recorded in
# token_usage since the subscription's current period started, across EVERY
# AI surface (chat turns AND email classify/magics/person-extraction all
# draw from the same pool — running low on email autopilot can gate chat
# too, and vice versa). No separate grant/reset step exists any more: a
# renewal just advances the period-start timestamp, which is what makes last
# period's leftover stop counting. BYOK also needs a (smaller) subscription
# once its price is configured — see STRIPE_PRICE_ID_BYOK below — but never
# draws on this allowance at all.
BILLING_ENABLED=false
# ADR 0195/0196 — a placeholder allowance pending a real pricing decision
# (owner formula: subscription price = fixed hosting/WhatsApp cost + K × tokens).
MONTHLY_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE=20000000
# Cheap free-tier model, OpenAI-compatible (Groq / DeepInfra / OpenRouter /
# Ollama / vLLM). Leave OSS_API_KEY blank to keep everyone on the premium model.
OSS_BASE_URL=
OSS_MODEL=
OSS_API_KEY=

# ── bot persona (ADR 0026) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read once at first boot to seed the 'default' row in `bot_personas`.
# The DB row is the source of truth thereafter — change identity by
# editing the row, not by editing these env vars.
#
# Two app instances sharing the same external chat (e.g. two Telegram
# bots in one group) should set distinct BOT_DISPLAY_NAME +
# BOT_MENTION_ALIASES so users can address each separately. Each
# instance also needs its own TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN.
BOT_PERSONA_ID=default
BOT_DISPLAY_NAME=Chord
# Comma-separated. Each alias becomes a literal users can type to
# address the bot (`@chord` / `@chord-beta`). Lowercase. Used to build
# the in-app mention/imperative/confirm regexes and the `/<alias> pair`
# slash command. Avoid spaces.
BOT_MENTION_ALIASES=chord
# What the system prompt says ("You are <BOT_PROMPT_NAME>, …"). Empty
# = same as BOT_DISPLAY_NAME.
BOT_PROMPT_NAME=
# Optional per-persona overrides for the LLM provider/model. Empty =
# fall back to the deploy-wide LLM_PROVIDER / *_MODEL above.
# NOTE: not yet wired — these values are stored on the persona row but
# the LLM factory still reads LLM_PROVIDER / OPENAI_MODEL / GEMINI_MODEL.
# Setting them has no effect today; they're stubbed for Phase 2.
BOT_LLM_PROVIDER=
BOT_MODEL=
# Comma-separated list of tool names from server/src/services/bot/tools.ts.
# Empty = all tools enabled. Use this + BOT_WRITE_ALLOWED to build a
# read-only persona for staging or for an "ask-only" variant.
BOT_ENABLED_TOOLS=
BOT_WRITE_ALLOWED=true

# Post-invocation follow-up window (ADR 0051). After the bot is @-mentioned in a
# group, it keeps replying to the GROUP's next N messages (from any member,
# group-scoped) without a re-mention — natural back-and-forth. Set
# BOT_FOLLOWUP_TURNS=0 to disable (invocation-only); values above 20 are clamped
# to 20 (hard cap). BOT_FOLLOWUP_TTL_MINUTES expires an idle window so a much
# later, unrelated message isn't answered out of the blue.
BOT_FOLLOWUP_TURNS=3
BOT_FOLLOWUP_TTL_MINUTES=10

# ── IM connectors (ADR 0010) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Telegram is the only deploy-wide connector. Slack/Discord deploy-wide
# wiring was removed (ADR 0154); the connector code stays for BYO tokens
# (ADR 0025), so no SLACK_* / DISCORD_* env vars.

# Telegram bot token from @BotFather. Required step after creating the bot:
# /setprivacy → Disable (otherwise the bot only sees /commands in groups,
# not regular text). v1 uses long-poll, no public URL needed.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# ── WhatsApp via Twilio (ADR 0053) — OPTIONAL, LOCAL DEV ONLY ─────────
# ⚠ PRODUCTION DOES NOT USE TWILIO. Deployment goes direct to Meta's Cloud
# API — set WHATSAPP_PROVIDER=cloud and the META_* keys in the ADR 0090
# block at the BOTTOM of this file. Everything in this section is then
# IGNORED: only one WhatsApp connector registers, chosen by that setting.
#
# This whole section is optional. Leave it empty unless you specifically
# want the Twilio Sandbox for local development, which is its one remaining
# use: the sandbox needs no Meta approval, so it is the quickest way to
# exercise the WhatsApp path on a laptop.
#
# (Twilio is NOT fully gone from the project: Twilio Verify
# still uses Twilio Verify for SMS OTP — ADR 0064, a separate product that
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID/AUTH_TOKEN below also feed. If you use that, keep
# those two set even with WHATSAPP_PROVIDER=cloud.)
#
# Enabled only when SID + auth token + sender are all set. Inbound is a
# webhook (POST /api/v1/webhooks/whatsapp), so unlike Telegram it needs a
# public URL. For local dev use the Twilio WhatsApp Sandbox + an https
# tunnel (ngrok), and point the sandbox "When a message comes in" webhook
# at https://<tunnel>/api/v1/webhooks/whatsapp.
#
# TWILIO_WHATSAPP_FROM is the sender in `whatsapp:+E164` form (the sandbox
# number is whatsapp:+14155238886). TWILIO_PUBLIC_URL is the public base
# (no trailing slash) the inbound signature is verified against — it MUST
# match what Twilio calls, including scheme/host. Set TWILIO_VALIDATE_SIGNATURE=0
# ONLY for tunnel dev where the URL is rewritten; never in production.
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=ACxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=your_twilio_auth_token_here
TWILIO_WHATSAPP_FROM=+14155238886
TWILIO_PUBLIC_URL=https://wa-dev.chordapp.ai
TWILIO_VALIDATE_SIGNATURE=1

# WhatsApp "taster" (ADR 0053): how many bot turns an unsigned WhatsApp
# stranger may use under a shadow account before the app-first signup gate
# kicks in. Each turn is a billed LLM call (and possibly a paid WhatsApp
# conversation), so this caps cost/abuse while still letting people try the
# bot first. On Google sign-in the shadow's data merges into the real
# account. 0 = disabled (hard gate from the first message).
WHATSAPP_TASTER_TURNS=10

# Content API quick-reply template SIDs (ADR 0053 §7), one per button count.
# In the Twilio Content Template Builder create a `twilio/quick-reply` template
# with body {{1}} and N buttons whose id+title are variables — 2-button:
# id {{2}}/title {{3}}, id {{4}}/title {{5}}; 3-button adds id {{6}}/title {{7}}.
# In-session quick-reply needs NO Meta approval. Paste the HX… SIDs here.
# Unset → action buttons (e.g. proposal confirm/reject) degrade to plain text.
# NOT needed under WHATSAPP_PROVIDER=cloud: Meta sends interactive buttons
# free-form inside the 24h window, so there is no template to configure.
TWILIO_QUICK_REPLY_SID_2=
TWILIO_QUICK_REPLY_SID_3=

# ── Phone identity + contact discovery (ADR 0064 / 0065) ──────────────
# OTP provider for phone verification. 'mock' (default) is a deterministic
# DB-backed store so the verify flow runs with no Twilio account (tests, mock
# demos). 'twilio' uses Twilio Verify (reuses TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID/AUTH_TOKEN)
# and needs the Verify service SID below.
# Twilio Verify service SID (VA…). Phone verification (ADR 0064) is ON iff this
# is set alongside TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID/AUTH_TOKEN, and OFF otherwise — there is
# deliberately no fixed-code fallback (the mock provider was removed).
TWILIO_VERIFY_SERVICE_SID=
# The fixed code the mock provider "sends" (never a real SMS). Demo/test only.
# Pepper folded into the phone HMAC used for contact matching. The SAME value
# must be built into the mobile client so client- and server-side hashes line
# up. This is a shared build-time constant, NOT an enclave secret — hashed
# discovery is deliberately not cryptographically private (see ADR 0065); the
# pepper only stops a raw DB dump from being rainbow-attacked. Override in prod.
CONTACT_HASH_PEPPER=chord-dev-contact-pepper

# ── Welcome DM (Telegram connector, app-first model) ──────────────────
# When an un-paired Telegram user DMs the bot, the server replies once
# with a short nudge: "Sign up at SIGNUP_URL, then paste your pair code
# from Settings → Connectors → Telegram."
#
# Defaults: if SIGNUP_URL is empty the server derives a default from
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE below (same host, port 8081 = Expo dev). Set it
# explicitly when deploying to a real domain.
#
# WELCOME_DM_ENABLED=0 restores the legacy silent-drop behaviour.
# WELCOME_DM_REGREET_DAYS gates how often the nudge re-fires for the
# same stranger.
SIGNUP_URL=
WELCOME_DM_ENABLED=1
WELCOME_DM_REGREET_DAYS=7

# Testing helper (AUTH_MODE=google only): JSON array of
# (telegram_id, email) pairs pre-seeded at server boot. Each entry
# creates a Chord user (no google_sub yet) + a telegram
# external_identities row. On first Google login with the matching
# email, the existing user is claimed in place (google_sub set,
# email_verified=1) — no pair-code dance needed for that dev's TG
# account. Idempotent: existing rows are reused. Leave empty to skip.
#   SEED_TG_EMAIL_PAIRS=[{"tg_id":"12345","email":"foo@example.com","display_name":"Foo"}]
SEED_TG_EMAIL_PAIRS=

# ── mobile (Expo) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Base URL the Expo app will call. For phone on LAN, set to your machine's IP.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE=http://192.168.1.101:4000

# ══ NEW KEYS ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Convention (2026-07-19): every newly introduced env var is APPENDED here,
# dated, with when-you-need-it and why-it-exists — so after a `git pull`,
# reading this section top-to-bottom answers "what do I need to add to my
# .env now". Fold an entry up into its topical section above once it has
# been around a while. Defaults are always safe: unset = feature off /
# previous behavior.

# ── added 2026-07-18, subscriptions 2026-09-22 · Stripe checkout
#    (ADR 0075/0195) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHEN: only when selling subscriptions (BILLING_ENABLED=true deploys).
# WHY: "Subscribe" opens a hosted Stripe Checkout in `mode: 'subscription'`;
# the webhook links the customer on checkout, then grants/renews the token
# allowance on every `invoice.paid` (first period and every renewal alike).
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY/WEBHOOK_SECRET/PRICE_ID must all be set for the system
# plan's Subscribe button to appear; the webhook 404s until the secret is
# set, so an unconfigured deploy exposes no purchase surface at all.
# STRIPE_PRICE_ID is the system-key plan (base cost + token allowance, one
# price — the $ amount lives in Stripe's dashboard, not here).
# STRIPE_PRICE_ID_BYOK is a SEPARATE Stripe product/price: the smaller
# base-only plan a BYOK user pays (covers Chord's hosting/WhatsApp cost,
# not AI cost — their own key covers that). Left unset, BYOK stays free
# exactly as it always has — X is a placeholder until this gets configured.
# Local test-mode: `stripe listen --forward-to
# localhost:4000/api/v1/webhooks/stripe` prints the whsec_… value.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
STRIPE_PRICE_ID=
STRIPE_PRICE_ID_BYOK=

# ── added 2026-07-18 · web origin for redirects (ADR 0075) ───────────
# WHEN: production deploys (set to https://chordapp.ai); local dev can
# leave it empty.
# WHY: absolute origin for Stripe success/cancel redirect URLs (and future
# absolute links). Empty → derived from SIGNUP_URL's origin.
WEB_ORIGIN=

# ── added 2026-07-18 · people/family facts from email (ADR 0076) ─────
# WHEN: opt-in per deploy; start with dogfood accounts.
# WHY: mines non-bulk Gmail-mirror threads for people, relationships, and
# birthdays → staged proposals in Memory / Action Items (one-time
# high-confidence auto-write bootstrap on a user's first pass). Costs one
# LLM call per non-bulk thread, hence OFF by default.
EMAIL_PERSONS_ENABLED=false

# ── added 2026-07-24 · additional mailboxes (ADR 0080) ────────────────
# WHEN: to let users attach Outlook.com / Microsoft 365 mailboxes. Create
# one multi-tenant app in Entra ID ("accounts in any org directory +
# personal Microsoft accounts"), add the web redirect URI, and set both.
# Empty → the "Add Outlook / Microsoft 365" option is hidden.
# WHY: Outlook mail syncs via Microsoft Graph with its own OAuth client;
# Google stays untouched. Yahoo/AOL/iCloud need NO keys — they attach via
# a user-generated IMAP app password.
MS_CLIENT_ID=
MS_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── added 2026-07-24 · mock seams for attached mailboxes (ADR 0080) ───
# WHEN: tests/demos without real accounts (mirrors GMAIL_PROVIDER=mock).
# WHY: 'mock' serves deterministic fixture mailboxes so connect → sync →
# reply → archive run keyless end-to-end.
OUTLOOK_PROVIDER=real
IMAP_PROVIDER=real

# ── added 2026-07-28 · Android Google sign-in (ADR 0081) ──────────────
# WHEN: nothing new to add — this note exists because the Android keys
# that used to live above are GONE. If your .env still sets
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_ANDROID or
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI_NATIVE, delete them; both were dead
# (never read by any code) and the first one is now actively misleading.
# WHY: Google no longer supports custom-scheme redirects on Android OAuth
# clients, so Android does not get a client of its own — it runs the web
# client's browser flow and catches the redirect at
# <EXPO_PUBLIC_WEB_BASE>/nativeauth via a verified App Link. Make sure
# EXPO_PUBLIC_WEB_BASE is set for any Android build (all three eas.json
# profiles already set it) and that /nativeauth is registered on the web
# client's Authorized Redirect URIs list.

# ── 2026-08-20 — TWILIO_NUDGE_TEMPLATE_SID (ADR 0089) ─────────────────
# TWILIO ONLY. Under WHATSAPP_PROVIDER=cloud this is ignored — the Meta
# equivalent is META_NUDGE_TEMPLATE_NAME in the ADR 0090 block below.
# WHEN: you run WhatsApp with a REAL (non-sandbox) sender and want the
# daily briefing to survive WhatsApp's 24-hour session window. Leave it
# empty otherwise — including for sandbox dev, where it is not needed.
# WHY: WhatsApp only delivers free-form text within 24h of the USER's
# last message. The briefing is proactive and fires at 07:00, so for
# anyone who did not write yesterday it is undeliverable as text. With
# this set, Chord instead sends this approved template — a quick reply
# asking whether they still want the briefing — and holds the briefing
# until they tap. Yes delivers it (their tap reopens the window); No
# mutes WhatsApp briefings for that channel only (in-app and push keep
# working). Unanswered, we never ask again until they write first.
# Build it in the Twilio Content Template Builder as a `twilio/quick-reply`
# with body {{1}} and two buttons ({{2}}/{{3}} and {{4}}/{{5}}), then
# submit it for META APPROVAL — unlike TWILIO_QUICK_REPLY_SID_*, this one
# is business-initiated and will not deliver until approved. Unset, the
# proactive send outside the window is skipped with a log line.
TWILIO_NUDGE_TEMPLATE_SID=

# ── 2026-08-20 — WhatsApp direct via Meta Cloud API (ADR 0090) ────────
# WHEN: your number is already on a WhatsApp Business Account and Twilio
# refuses to register it as a sender ("Oops, something went wrong"). A
# number belongs to exactly ONE WABA, so Twilio cannot claim one that is
# already attached — Meta's Cloud API talks to that same WABA directly,
# with no migration and no release cooldown.
# WHY: also removes the BSP margin, and in-session quick-reply buttons
# stop needing a pre-approved Content template (only the ADR 0089
# out-of-window nudge still needs an approved one, addressed by NAME).
#
# Leave WHATSAPP_PROVIDER=twilio (the default) for local dev — the Twilio
# sandbox needs no Meta approval and is still the easiest way to develop.
# Twilio Verify (TWILIO_VERIFY_SERVICE_SID, ADR 0064) is a SEPARATE product
# and is unaffected by this setting.
#
# Where each value comes from:
#   META_WHATSAPP_TOKEN            System User permanent token, with
#                                  whatsapp_business_messaging. Business
#                                  Settings -> System Users -> Generate.
#   META_WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID  WhatsApp Manager -> API Setup. This is
#                                  an opaque NUMERIC ID, not the number.
#   META_APP_SECRET                App Dashboard -> Settings -> Basic.
#                                  Verifies X-Hub-Signature-256.
#   META_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN      Any string you invent; paste the same
#                                  one into the webhook config so Meta's
#                                  GET handshake matches.
#   META_NUDGE_TEMPLATE_NAME       An APPROVED quick-reply template. As of
#                                  ADR 0091 it carries the BRIEFING, not a
#                                  yes/no question: FIVE body variables and
#                                  two buttons. The exact body to submit is
#                                  in the NEW KEYS block at the bottom of
#                                  this file. The old `morning_nudge`
#                                  (text-only, no buttons) will NOT work.
# Webhook URL to register with Meta: https://<host>/api/v1/webhooks/whatsapp
#
# >>> TO USE META, SET THIS TO `cloud`. The `twilio` below is the DEFAULT
# >>> (kept so existing deploys and sandbox dev keep working) — not a
# >>> recommendation. Nothing else in this block is read unless it is `cloud`.
WHATSAPP_PROVIDER=twilio
META_WHATSAPP_TOKEN=
META_WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
META_APP_SECRET=
META_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN=
META_GRAPH_VERSION=v21.0
META_NUDGE_TEMPLATE_NAME=
META_NUDGE_TEMPLATE_LANG=en_US

# ── 2026-08-21 — Weather in the briefing + IP location (ADR 0091) ─────
# WHEN: you want the morning briefing to open with a local forecast, and
# you want the out-of-window WhatsApp template to carry real content.
# Both are optional; unset, the briefing is exactly what it is today.
# WHY: ADR 0089's out-of-window template asked "want your briefing?" and
# carried nothing. It now mirrors the briefing itself — five single-line
# facts plus links — because a Meta template variable cannot hold a
# newline, so the shape has to be baked into the approved body.
#
# These two keys are INDEPENDENT and use unrelated services. Weather needs
# no credential at all; only the optional IP-location tier does.
#
# WEATHER_PROVIDER: '' disables the weather line. 'open-meteo' needs NO API
# key and no signup — there is no key env var because the service has none;
# set this to `open-meteo` and it works. Its free tier covers
# non-commercial use up to ~10k calls/day, and Chord makes roughly one call
# per user per morning, so the ceiling is not the concern — the licence is:
# once Chord charges for the product (ADR 0075/0079) "non-commercial" stops
# applying and it needs a paid plan or a different provider. That is what
# the seam is for. 'mock' returns a fixture and never touches the network,
# for tests and mock-LLM demos.
WEATHER_PROVIDER=
#
# GEOIP_DB_PATH: OPTIONAL, and nothing to do with the weather service above
# — weather works fine without it, just with a coarser location. This is a
# MaxMind GeoLite2-City .mmdb, which is where MAXMIND_LICENSE_KEY comes in:
# free, but MaxMind requires a signup + EULA to DOWNLOAD the file.
#
# Weather needs a location and Chord deliberately never asks for GPS, so it
# estimates one — first from the request IP against THIS LOCAL FILE (the
# address never leaves the server and is never stored, only the city it
# produced), then from the user's timezone, and finally from whatever they
# set in Settings → Daily briefing, which always wins. Leave empty to skip
# the IP tier; a timezone is a coarse proxy (America/New_York spans Boston
# to Miami) but the Settings override covers it. deploy.sh downloads and
# uploads the file when MAXMIND_LICENSE_KEY is set in deploy.env.
GEOIP_DB_PATH=
#
# ── The Meta template to submit (ADR 0091) ────────────────────────────
# Name it whatever you set in META_NUDGE_TEMPLATE_NAME, category UTILITY,
# language en_US, with TWO quick-reply buttons: "Full briefing" and
# "Stop these". Body EXACTLY:
#
#   Morning! Here's what's on your radar:
#
#   {{1}}
#
#   ⏰ This week:
#   {{2}}
#   chordapp.ai/calendar
#
#   📋 Heads-up:
#   {{3}}
#
#   ⏰ Open tasks:
#   {{4}}
#   chordapp.ai/catchup/tasks
#
#   📬 Email — waiting on you:
#   {{5}}
#   chordapp.ai/email
#
# There are FIVE variables — {{1}}..{{5}} — filled per user each morning:
# weather / this week / heads-up / open tasks / email. Chord never sends an
# empty one (Meta rejects empty parameters) and never one containing a
# newline, which is why the section headers and blank lines are part of the
# approved body rather than something a variable could carry.
#
# The three chordapp.ai URLs are deliberately LITERAL text, not variables.
# They are identical for every user, so making them dynamic would buy
# nothing and would show Meta's reviewer a template capable of sending an
# arbitrary link — fixed URLs review more cleanly and cannot drift.

# ── 2026-08-21 — stale to-do closure (ADR 0092) ──────────────────────────
# When you need it: never, unless a week feels wrong. Ships with a default.
# Why it exists: an open task carrying a due date auto-archives this many
# days after that date (recoverable, shows in To Do's -> Archived). Only
# DATED tasks are eligible — an undated one is never auto-archived however
# old, and a task with a reminder still pending waits for it to fire.
# Archiving fires at most once per task, so anything the user pulls back
# out of Archived stays out. Set to a large number to effectively disable.
TASK_OVERDUE_ARCHIVE_DAYS=7

# ── 2026-08-22 — briefing timezone fallback (ADR 0098) ───────────────────
# When you need it: only if your users are mostly NOT US-Eastern. Ships
# with a default.
# Why it exists: a user's briefing fires at their nudge time in their own
# IANA zone, learned from the app (`users.last_tz`). Someone who has never
# opened a client has no zone on file, and the fallback used to be the
# SERVER's clock — so on a UTC VM a 07:00 briefing was queued for 07:00Z,
# which is midnight in Los Angeles. This is the zone used when we've never
# seen the user's own. Any valid IANA zone; an invalid value falls back to
# UTC. Real users' zones always win, and their first app open re-times the
# briefing.
BRIEFING_DEFAULT_TZ=America/New_York

# ── 2026-08-25 — reading the actual email (ADR 0110) ───────────────────
# When you need it: only to tune cost. All three ship with defaults.
# Why they exist: the AI passes were reading a ~200-char preview, most of
# which was invisible marketing padding, because sync fetched headers only.
# Sync now keeps a bounded body excerpt and the passes read that.
#
# DEEP_READ_PER_TICK: threads per magics tick allowed a SECOND read of the
# whole message, when the model says the detail it needed was cut off (an
# invoice total, an itinerary). A runaway BACKSTOP, not a ration - it defaults
# to the magics thread cap so it never refuses a genuine request, since a
# refused one is how an invoice went unread with the amount a single word past
# the excerpt. A thread is mined once ever, so the real bound is one extra call
# per thread. 0 disables escalation entirely. DEEP_READ_CHARS is how much that
# second read shows: past prod's median body (9.2k) and mean (18.1k), short of
# the p90 (47k), which is marketing footer.
EMAIL_DEEP_READ_PER_TICK=25
EMAIL_DEEP_READ_CHARS=24000
#
# BODY_BACKFILL_PER_TICK: messages per sync whose body is fetched to fill a
# missing excerpt. Mail that arrived before this change has none, and
# re-classifying it on the snippet alone just repeats the wrong answer. Each
# filled message re-queues its thread for classification. 0 disables the
# backfill without disabling capture on new mail.
EMAIL_BODY_BACKFILL_PER_TICK=25

# ── 2026-08-25 — the out-of-window ask expires (ADR 0089 §6 addendum) ───
# When you need it: only to change how often a silent WhatsApp channel is
# re-asked, or to switch the behaviour off. Ships with a default.
# Why it exists: when the 24h session window is closed, the briefing goes
# out as an approved template asking whether the user still wants it. ADR
# 0089 asked exactly once and then stayed quiet until the user wrote to the
# bot of their own accord — which in production meant one user got nothing
# for days, indistinguishable from a broken briefing. Each ask is a billed
# business-initiated conversation, so it must not repeat daily; this is how
# many days an unanswered ask keeps the channel quiet before we may ask
# again. 0 (or less) restores ask-once-forever.
WHATSAPP_NUDGE_REASK_DAYS=7

# --- 2026-08-25 (ADR 0115) ---
# How long a sent message is held before it actually goes, so the sender can
# undo it. The hold is server-side: an app closed during the window still
# sends. Set to 0 to send immediately - that also turns the outbox scheduler
# off and removes the Undo button rather than showing one that does nothing.
EMAIL_UNDO_SECONDS=8
# How often the outbox looks for messages whose hold has expired. Its own
# tick rather than the 15s proactive one, which would turn an 8s window into
# anywhere from 8 to 23 seconds.
EMAIL_OUTBOX_TICK_MS=2000

# --- 2026-08-25 (ADR 0118) ---
# Real push: Gmail users.watch, Graph subscriptions, IMAP IDLE. OFF by
# default because it needs a publicly reachable HTTPS callback plus setup at
# each provider - and polling carries every mailbox without it, so turning
# this on only makes mail arrive sooner.
MAIL_PUSH_ENABLED=0
# Public base URL the providers call back on, e.g. https://api.chordapp.ai
MAIL_PUSH_PUBLIC_URL=
# Gmail only: its Pub/Sub payload carries no secret, so the callback URL
# carries this one. It guards against being a free way to make the server
# sync - the notification itself has no content.
MAIL_PUSH_GMAIL_TOKEN=
# The Pub/Sub topic users.watch publishes to, and which must have a push
# subscription pointed at <MAIL_PUSH_PUBLIC_URL>/api/v1/webhooks/mail/gmail
MAIL_PUSH_GMAIL_TOPIC=
# How often to renew registrations that are close to expiring (Gmail 7 days,
# Graph ~3), well before the deadline so a failed renewal has retries left.
MAIL_PUSH_RENEW_TICK_MS=900000
# IMAP IDLE holds one login open per account - FEWER logins than polling,
# but a long-lived resource, so it is capped and separately switchable.
IMAP_IDLE_ENABLED=1
IMAP_IDLE_MAX_ACCOUNTS=50
IMAP_IDLE_REFRESH_MS=600000

# — 2026-08-25 —
# ADR 0127. The bulk-mail deny-list for the AI passes (magics, person
# extraction, attachment import). ON by default. Turn it off when you need
# to tell a missing suggestion from a model that declined — that is the
# whole reason this is a flag and not a constant.
EMAIL_BULK_GATE=true

# — 2026-08-25: the mail surface is ON by default —
# Owner decision. A fresh install now reads mail AND reasons about it:
#   GMAIL_ENABLED=true, EMAIL_CLASSIFIER=llm,
#   EMAIL_MAGICS_ENABLED=true, EMAIL_PERSONS_ENABLED=true
# Set any of them to false/empty to opt a deployment back out. Per-user AI
# cost is real from the first sync, bounded by the ADR 0079 entitlement gate
# (an unentitled user skips the AI passes) and the ADR 0127 bulk gate.
# MAIL_PUSH_ENABLED stays OFF: that is not a product call, it is a feature
# nobody has verified end-to-end against a real Pub/Sub topic.

# — 2026-08-25 —
# ADR 0136. Serve remote email images through our own fetcher, so the sender
# meets our server instead of a family's phone (no IP, device or cross-sender
# correlation) and the mail renders instead of looking broken. ON by default.
# Set false when the bandwidth this costs bites: readers fall back to ADR
# 0120's block-and-offer banner, which still works. Per-user override lives in
# Settings (users.email_images_auto), which this flag overrides deployment-wide.
EMAIL_IMAGE_PROXY=true

# — 2026-08-31 —
# ADR 0143. How old a READ email may be and still be mined for to-dos.
# Unread mail is exempt at any age — nobody has looked at it yet, so its
# obligation still stands. This bounds only the read-AND-old combination,
# which is how someone signals they already dealt with it. Deliberately far
# shorter than GMAIL_BACKFILL_DAYS (90): that window exists so search has
# history, which is a different job from deciding what is still owed.
# Raise it if suggestions are being missed; lower it if a connect still
# produces a pile of settled to-dos.
EMAIL_MINE_READ_MAX_AGE_DAYS=7

# — 2026-09-01 —
# ADR 0144. Background mirror sweep. Before this, mail synced ONLY when the
# Email tab was focused (plus once a day inside the morning briefing), so
# every other surface — tiles, unread counts, the decisions queue, To Do's —
# rendered whatever was true when that screen was last open, and mail read or
# deleted in Gmail stayed stale indefinitely. It also meant ADR 0117's
# new-mail notification could effectively only fire while the user was
# already looking at their mail.
#
# Runs at `mirror` depth: body excerpts, the new-mail notification and the
# vacation responder, then STOPS at the same line ADR 0079's entitlement gate
# draws. It never reaches classification, magics or person extraction, so
# sweeping more often cannot multiply LLM spend — only provider calls.
MAIL_SWEEP_ENABLED=true
# How stale a user's mirror may get before the sweep picks them up.
MAIL_SWEEP_MINUTES=5
# Users per proactive tick, stalest-first. Bounds the burst without starving
# anyone: with more users than this, everyone still comes up, just less often.
# Lower it if provider quota or IMAP login rate (ADR 0080) bites.
MAIL_SWEEP_BATCH=20

# — 2026-09-07 —
# ADR 0148. Proactive weather-location check. Chord never had a way to notice
# its stored weather location had gone stale (someone travels, or set the
# wrong city once). It now compares a fresh IP / timezone fix against the
# stored location during the morning briefing and, when they disagree by
# more than WEATHER_DRIFT_KM, appends one line asking whether to switch —
# the user replies in chat and the bot's set_weather_location tool acts on it.
# No silent changes. Needs WEATHER_PROVIDER set to do anything; the IP arm
# also needs GEOIP_DB_PATH (else it falls back to the coarser timezone city).
WEATHER_DRIFT_KM=100
# Wait this long before asking again about the same place after a prompt goes
# unanswered or the user says "keep it". A move to a genuinely new location
# (again >WEATHER_DRIFT_KM away) resets this and can ask sooner.
WEATHER_DRIFT_REASK_DAYS=7

# — 2026-09-10 —
# ADR 0156. Sign in with Apple on native iOS — required on the public App
# Store because Chord offers Google sign-in (Apple guideline 4.8). You need
# these when shipping an iOS build to the App Store; leave them empty
# everywhere else. The feature is ON only when all three secrets are set
# (and AUTH_MODE=google): the server signs a client secret from the key both
# to exchange the app's sign-in code and to revoke the user's Apple token on
# account deletion, which Apple requires. Until then /health reports
# appleSignIn=false and the app shows no Apple button.
# Apple Developer → Membership: the 10-character team id.
APPLE_TEAM_ID=
# Apple Developer → Keys → a key with "Sign in with Apple" enabled: its id.
APPLE_KEY_ID=
# That key's .p8 file: the base64 lines BETWEEN "-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----"
# and "-----END PRIVATE KEY-----", joined onto one line with no quotes. That
# form needs no escapes, so systemd's env-file parser can't mangle it.
APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY=
# The iOS bundle id Apple issued the sign-in for (mobile/app.json).
APPLE_BUNDLE_ID=app.chord.demo

# — 2026-09-11 —
# ADR 0158. Route the body of a `travel/*` email through the existing trip
# classifier and stage a create_trip + legs/accommodation proposal bundle in
# Action Items — so a Booking.com / Expedia confirmation with no PDF still
# becomes a trip. You don't need to add anything: it's ON by default. Set to
# false to disable (it costs one extra classifier LLM call per travel-category
# thread, once, until marked). Only meaningful with EMAIL_MAGICS_ENABLED on.
EMAIL_TRIPS_ENABLED=true

# — 2026-09-11 —
# ADR 0162/0163. Calendar events remember what led to them. Both are ON by
# default; you don't need to add anything unless you want to disable one.
# EVENT_CONTEXT_EXCERPT_ENABLED auto-fills a new event's description with a
# link/excerpt back to the email or chat message that created it — free,
# deterministic, no LLM call. Set false to turn it off (e.g. you don't want
# every Chord-created event's Google description touched).
EVENT_CONTEXT_EXCERPT_ENABLED=true
# MEETING_CONTEXT_ENABLED mines a short "what this meeting is about" from
# prior email correspondence with an invited attendee, once, when an invite
# is sent — costs one small LLM call ONLY when matching mail is actually
# found (most events have no invitees and cost nothing). Set false to
# disable if you want zero LLM spend from calendar invites.
MEETING_CONTEXT_ENABLED=true

# — 2026-09-11 — Live web search for the bot (`search_web` tool)
# WHEN: you want the bot to answer "what's a good restaurant in X" / "tell me
# about this city" with a real lookup instead of only its own training
# knowledge. Empty (default) disables the tool entirely — the bot is told
# live search is unavailable and answers from general knowledge only, never
# claiming to have searched.
# 'mock' returns fixture results, no key needed (tests, LLM_PROVIDER=mock
# demos). 'google' calls the real Google Custom Search JSON API — create a
# Custom Search Engine at https://programmablesearchengine.google.com (set it
# to search the whole web) for GOOGLE_SEARCH_CX, and an API key with the
# Custom Search API enabled at https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
# for GOOGLE_SEARCH_API_KEY. Same GCP project as GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID works fine.
SEARCH_PROVIDER=
GOOGLE_SEARCH_API_KEY=
GOOGLE_SEARCH_CX=

# — 2026-09-15 — Redis for cross-process SSE fanout + the BullMQ job queue
# WHEN: you're running more than one Node process (a web + worker split, or
# more than one web instance) and need the SSE hub to fan a broadcast out to
# every process, or you want bot-turn/mail-sync/proactive-scan work to run
# through a real job queue instead of inline. Empty (default) disables both:
# the SSE hub stays in-process-only (ADR 0002) and every queue falls back to
# running its work inline, exactly as before either existed. Local dev, CI,
# and the mock-LLM sim (`npm run sim`) need no Redis at all.
# WHY: the SSE hub and the AI-work queues share one Redis instance rather
# than introducing two pieces of new infra. Point this at a LOCAL Redis
# (`redis://127.0.0.1:6379`) if self-hosting on the same VM — bind Redis to
# 127.0.0.1 only, it should never be reachable from outside the box.
REDIS_URL=

# — 2026-09-15 — Web/worker process split (ADR 0172)
# WHEN: you're tuning how many Postgres connections the server holds open.
# Default 10 is unchanged from before the split; you don't need to add this
# unless you're raising or lowering it.
# WHY: the server now runs as two processes in production — a "web" role
# (entrypoints/web.ts: the HTTP API) and a "worker" role
# (entrypoints/worker.ts: the proactive scheduler, mail sync, push
# publisher) — each opening its OWN Postgres pool of this size. Total
# connections against Postgres = (number of running processes) x
# DB_POOL_MAX. Check the VM's configured max_connections before raising
# this. Local dev/CI/sim still run both roles in one process
# (`server.ts`), so this rarely needs to change there.
DB_POOL_MAX=10

# — 2026-09-15 — Mail sync on a BullMQ queue (ADR pending)
# WHEN: you're tuning how many mailboxes sync in parallel during the
# background sweep. Only meaningful with REDIS_URL set — unset, sweeps
# stay fully sequential (one mailbox at a time), unchanged from before.
# WHY: MAIL_SWEEP_BATCH still bounds how many stale mailboxes are
# considered per tick; this bounds how many of them actually sync at
# once. Raising it trades faster catch-up for more concurrent
# Gmail/IMAP/LLM-classifier calls hitting provider rate limits at once —
# watch headroom before raising past the default.
MAIL_SYNC_QUEUE_CONCURRENCY=3

# — 2026-09-15 — Bot turns on a BullMQ queue (ADR 0178)
# WHEN: you're tuning how many bot turns (LLM streams) can run at once
# process-wide, or how long a stalled-job check waits before treating a
# turn as dead. Only meaningful with REDIS_URL set — unset, bot turns run
# exactly as before (one fire-and-forget call per turn, no concurrency
# cap).
# WHY: without a cap, a burst of messages across many groups launches one
# concurrent LLM stream per group with no limit. Raising CONCURRENCY
# trades faster reply latency under load for more simultaneous
# LLM-provider calls hitting rate limits at once. LOCK_MS must stay
# comfortably longer than the slowest realistic turn (up to 20 LLM
# round-trips) — this queue deliberately does NOT redeliver a stalled
# turn (see bot-turn.queue.ts), so a lock that expires too early fails a
# turn that was still legitimately running instead of retrying it.
BOT_TURN_QUEUE_CONCURRENCY=8
BOT_TURN_QUEUE_LOCK_MS=300000

# — 2026-09-17 — Read-time email magics (ADR 0180)
# WHEN: you want to turn OFF the extraction pass that runs the first time a
# user opens an email thread (bypasses the background sweep's bulk/
# discretionary/stale-read gates and its once-ever `magic` mark, so a
# just-opened thread that the sweep would have skipped can still surface a
# suggestion or a spam/phishing caution).
# WHY: unlike the other EMAIL_*_ENABLED flags, this one is NOT "usually
# free" — every not-yet-opened thread costs one real LLM call the first
# time it's read. Turn it off if that per-open cost is a problem; nothing
# else in the mail reader depends on it.
EMAIL_READ_TIME_MAGICS_ENABLED=true

# — 2026-09-17 — Background calendar sweep (ADR 0181)
# WHEN: you're tuning how quickly a Google-side calendar edit (e.g. an event
# moved to a different calendar) converges without the user triggering an
# opportunistic sync (a bot call, a foreground refresh). Turn ENABLED off to
# go back to purely opportunistic sync, unchanged from before this ADR.
# WHY: mirrors ADR 0144's mail sweep — two-way calendar sync otherwise only
# ran when something in the app happened to ask for it, so a change made
# directly in Google Calendar between sessions could sit unreconciled (a
# stray duplicate visible) indefinitely. MINUTES bounds staleness before a
# user is swept again; BATCH bounds how many stale users one tick considers.
CALENDAR_SWEEP_ENABLED=true
CALENDAR_SWEEP_MINUTES=5
CALENDAR_SWEEP_BATCH=20

# — 2026-09-17 — Gmail per-message fetch concurrency (performance audit)
# WHEN: backfill/incremental sync/junk-peek were each fetching messages one
# at a time (`getMessage` awaited sequentially in a loop) — on a cold
# connect that's up to ~2000 sequential round trips, wall-clock-bound by
# latency rather than by Gmail's quota (quota is charged per call either
# way, concurrent or not). Lower this if a single mailbox's sync is putting
# real pressure on Gmail's per-user rate limit.
# WHY: turns backfill time into (call count / this number) round trips
# instead of (call count) round trips — same total API cost, much less wall
# clock, which is what a mailbox-count-scaled sweep actually needs.
GMAIL_FETCH_CONCURRENCY=12

# — 2026-09-17 — Google Calendar push concurrency (performance audit)
# WHEN: `pushDirtyEvents` sent one dirty event to the Calendar API at a
# time, so a fanned-out group event (one row per member) or a bulk edit
# paid one HTTP round trip per row, back to back. Lower this if a single
# user's Calendar write quota is a concern; each row is still exactly one
# Calendar API call, just no longer serialized behind the others.
# WHY: same total request count, much less wall clock — writes are kept
# more conservative than the read-side GMAIL_FETCH_CONCURRENCY on purpose.
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_PUSH_CONCURRENCY=5

# — 2026-09-18 — Incidental-attachment retention sweep (bug/feature bundle,
# insurance-cards-file-link-bug)
# WHEN: raise/lower this if the household wants email-imported files (ADR
# 0071) to age out faster/slower before being deleted. A direct upload
# (chat, WhatsApp, the mobile file picker) is NEVER touched by this sweep —
# only attachments with origin_email_thread_id set (auto-imported from a
# synced mailbox) are eligible, and only when NOT pinned.
# WHY: nothing previously deleted an attachment on any cadence, so every
# marketing logo and coupon banner email auto-imports mint (ADR 0071) sat
# forever. The real value of that pipeline (tasks/events/facts) is already
# captured as durable rows by the time this sweep would run; the raw file
# copy is a byproduct, not the deliverable. Pin anything worth keeping past
# this window.
ATTACHMENT_RETENTION_DAYS=45

# — 2026-09-22 — Token usage counter (ADR 0193)
# WHEN: you want to change the "total" number the Settings token counter
# shows ("142K / 2M tokens this month"). Purely cosmetic — nothing reads
# this to gate access; the token balance (MONTHLY_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE /
# BILLING_ENABLED above) is still the only thing that gates AI features.
# BYOK users never see this counter at all (their key, their cost, nothing
# recorded — though BYOK may owe a separate base-plan subscription, ADR 0195).
# WHY: there was no way for a user (or you) to see how many tokens an
# account was actually burning across bot turns + email AI passes. This is
# a static placeholder denominator until real plan tiers exist.
TOKEN_QUOTA_PER_MONTH=2000000

# — 2026-09-18 — Itinerary attachment expiry (insurance-cards-file-link-bug)
# WHEN: raise/lower how long a boarding pass / hotel confirmation attachment
# survives after its trip is actually over. Applies regardless of upload
# origin or age (unlike ATTACHMENT_RETENTION_DAYS above, which is email-
# import-only) — a direct screenshot upload of a boarding pass is just as
# done once the flight lands. A round-trip ticket covering two legs in one
# attachment survives until the LATER leg passes; pinning still exempts it.
# WHY: the itinerary document's whole purpose is getting you through the
# trip — once the last leg lands / the stay checks out, it has no more
# reason to occupy storage than a delivered parcel does (ADR 0135's same
# reasoning, applied to the source file instead of a derived tile fact).
ATTACHMENT_ITINERARY_GRACE_DAYS=7

# — 2026-09-22 — Token-denominated billing (ADR 0194)
# WHEN: renamed from CREDITS_PER_BUNDLE — if your .env still sets that name,
# switch it to TOKENS_PER_BUNDLE (same billing-section entry above).
# WHY: a debit used to be a flat 1 "credit" per turn regardless of how many
# tokens that turn actually spent. It's now the turn's real token cost (or
# nothing, if a provider never reported usage) — so the unit sold and the
# unit spent are the same thing everywhere: TOKENS_PER_BUNDLE grants a
# token count, not an abstract "credit" count.

# — 2026-09-22 — Subscriptions, not bundles; BYOK pays a base fee too
#   (ADR 0195)
# WHEN: renamed AGAIN from TOKENS_PER_BUNDLE to MONTHLY_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE (see
# the billing section above), and a new STRIPE_PRICE_ID_BYOK exists.
# WHY: Stripe checkout moved from a one-time `mode: 'payment'` bundle to a
# recurring `mode: 'subscription'` — there's no "bundle" any more, only a
# monthly allowance that resets (never rolls over) each `invoice.paid`.
# Separately: BYOK is no longer unconditionally free once STRIPE_PRICE_ID_BYOK
# is set — Chord's hosting/WhatsApp costs exist regardless of whose model
# key answers a turn, so BYOK now needs its own (smaller) base subscription.
# Unset, BYOK behaves exactly as before this ADR: always free.

# — 2026-09-22 — Usage is the ledger (ADR 0196)
# WHEN: no new env var — this is a behavior note, not a config change. The
# `invoice.paid` renewal from ADR 0195 used to also call a "reset the
# balance" step; that step no longer exists.
# WHY: MONTHLY_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE now gates EVERY AI surface from one shared,
# COMPUTED balance (allowance minus token_usage since the period started) —
# not just chat turns. Before this, the email AI passes (classification,
# magics, person-extraction) checked entitlement as a yes/no gate but never
# actually drew down the balance, so a family that ran mostly on email
# automation could spend real money without it ever showing up as usage.
# There's nothing left to keep in sync manually: recording usage (which
# every AI-consuming call already did, for the Settings counter) now IS
# what determines when access gates.

# — 2026-09-23 — Prompt log no longer stores email text
# PROMPT_LOG=meta
# WHEN: nothing to do — the default is what you want. Set `full` in local
# dev only if you need to read whole prompts; it is ignored (treated as
# `meta`) when NODE_ENV=production. `off` disables the prompt log entirely.
# WHY: prompts.log used to write every bot turn's full prompt — email
# subjects from digest history, attachment excerpts — to an unrotated file,
# readable by anyone with shell access. It is now prompts-YYYY-MM-DD.log,
# kept 7 days, and records message roles and sizes only. In production the
# raw model-reply excerpts in the attachment/trip/content classifier
# warnings are also dropped (only lengths are logged).

# PUSH_EMAIL_PREVIEW=false
# WHEN: nothing to do — default keeps the new-mail push generic ("New mail" /
# "N new emails"). Set true only if you want the sender + subject in the
# notification, knowing the text then crosses Expo and APNs/FCM.
# WHY: a push body is mail content handed to third parties.

# — 2026-09-23 — Email and attachments sealed at rest (ADR 0201, phase 2 = files)
# CONTENT_ENCRYPTION=false
# WHEN: turn on AFTER the release that contains the decrypt plugin is running
# everywhere (web AND worker). Then run the backfill for what already exists:
#   npm run encrypt:backfill --workspace=server -- --step attachments
# Take a database backup and a copy of DATA_DIR/files first; it rewrites files.
# WHY: reads always open anything sealed and pass plaintext through, so this
# only decides whether NEW writes are sealed. Off = behaviour unchanged.
# CONTENT_KEY_PROVIDER=local        # local | gcp-kms
# CONTENT_KEK=                      # base64 32 bytes; required for `local` in production
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# GCP_KMS_KEY_NAME=                 # projects/P/locations/L/keyRings/R/cryptoKeys/K for gcp-kms
# WHEN: `local` keeps the key-encryption key in this file next to the database,
# so it protects nothing against someone on the VM. Use it to ship and test; use
# gcp-kms (service account with only cloudkms.cryptoKeyEncrypterDecrypter on
# that key, humans denied) for the protection this exists to give.
# CONTENT_DEK_TTL_MS=600000         # how long an unwrapped key stays in memory
# CONTENT_DEK_CACHE_MAX=5000
# CONTENT_KEY_POOL_MAX=2            # extra DB connections per process, for key lookups only
# WHY the extra pool: Kysely runs the read plugin while still holding the query's
# connection, so a key load through the app pool can deadlock or exhaust it.
# Total Postgres connections per process are now DB_POOL_MAX + CONTENT_KEY_POOL_MAX.

# — 2026-09-23 — Mail sealed at rest (ADR 0201, phase 3)
# CONTENT_LEGACY_TSV=true
# WHEN: leave on until the backfill has sealed and indexed the existing mailbox:
#   npm run encrypt:backfill --workspace=server -- --step email
#   npm run encrypt:backfill --workspace=server -- --step verify
# then set it to false on EVERY instance (web and worker), restart, and drop the
# old plaintext search columns:
#   npm run encrypt:backfill --workspace=server -- --step finalize-email --vacuum
# WHY: while it is on, search also reads the old Postgres-generated tsvector
# columns, but only for rows with no blind index, so mail that predates the
# index stays findable during rollout. Finalize refuses to run while it is on,
# because the code must stop reading a column before the column can go.
# Take a database backup first; the email step rewrites every mail row.

# — 2026-09-23 — Derived content sealed at rest (ADR 0201, phase 4)
# No new key. CONTENT_LEGACY_TSV (above) now also governs attachment chunks.
# WHEN: after phase 3's steps, and before dropping the old chunk index:
#   npm run encrypt:backfill --workspace=server -- --step derived
#   npm run encrypt:backfill --workspace=server -- --step verify-derived
# then, with CONTENT_LEGACY_TSV=false on every instance:
#   npm run encrypt:backfill --workspace=server -- --step finalize-derived --vacuum
# WHY: the text read out of files, suggestions mined from mail, drafts and
# meeting summaries are as sensitive as the mail and files they came from.
# Take a database backup first; the derived step rewrites those rows.

# — 2026-09-23 — Cloud SQL (ADR 0202): operator variables, read by scripts/deploy/cloudsql.sh, not by the server
# CLOUDSQL_APP_PASSWORD   # export in your shell; never write it to a file
# CLOUDSQL_REGION, CLOUDSQL_INSTANCE, CLOUDSQL_TIER, CLOUDSQL_STORAGE_GB, CLOUDSQL_KMS_KEYRING
# WHEN: only when you run the migration. See docs/ops/cloud-sql.md.

# — 2026-09-24 — Opening a thread no longer waits on the AI check for as long as it takes
# EMAIL_READ_TIME_WAIT_MS=1500
# WHEN: optional; the default is right for a real model. The first open of a thread runs one
# LLM check (spam/phishing caution, suggestions). The open waits up to this long for it, so a
# quick answer still shows its caution banner with the email, and otherwise shows the email at
# once and the check finishes behind it (the screen reloads when it does). 0 = never wait.
# The Email tab's sync also returns before its AI passes now; that has no setting.

# — 2026-09-24 — A mailbox whose sync fails is no longer retried on every tick
# MAIL_SYNC_BACKOFF_MAX_MINUTES=60
# WHEN: optional; the default is right. After a failed background sync the mailbox is left alone
# for 5 minutes (10 for a quota/rate-limit error), doubling per consecutive failure up to this
# many minutes, and forgotten on the next success. A sync the person asks for is never held back.
# 0 or below turns it off (retry every 15-second tick, as before the fix).

# — 2026-09-25 — The token master key behind Cloud KMS (ADR 0210, Phase 8a)
# MASTER_KEY_WRAPPED=            # printed by `npm run wrap:master-key` on the VM; wins over MASTER_KEY when set
# MASTER_KEY_KMS_KEY=            # projects/…/cryptoKeys/… that wrapped it; default GCP_KMS_KEY_NAME. NEVER change once set.
# WHEN: only when you run the cutover in docs/ops/master-key.md. Leave both unset in dev/CI (MASTER_KEY stays plain).
# Once MASTER_KEY_WRAPPED works in production, remove MASTER_KEY from .env.prod. Keep an offline copy of the plain key.

# — 2026-09-25 — The bot can look up a name in your phone's Contacts (ADR 0212)
# CONTACT_LOOKUP_TIMEOUT_MS=8000
# WHEN: optional; the default is right. Asking "what is Ben's email" in your 1:1 chat with Chord
# asks the Chord app on your phone to search its Contacts for that one name and send back only the
# match. This is how long the bot waits for the phone before saying it could not reach it (app
# closed, web, or WhatsApp). Nothing is stored unless you ask Chord to remember it.

# — 2026-09-27 — Email sent from chat is held a minute so "unsend that" can catch it (ADR 0214)
# EMAIL_CHAT_UNDO_SECONDS=60
# WHEN: optional; the default is right. The bot can now write and forward email; nothing goes
# until you tap Send on its card, and then it waits this long before leaving, so you can still
# type "unsend that". Separate from EMAIL_UNDO_SECONDS (the app composer's tap-to-undo, 8s),
# because typing takes longer than tapping. 0 = send the moment you confirm. The outbox now stays
# on unless BOTH values are 0, so setting EMAIL_UNDO_SECONDS=0 no longer strands chat mail.

# — 2026-10-01 — Sign in with Apple on the web login (ADR 0156 addendum)
# APPLE_SERVICES_ID=
# WHEN: only if you want the WEB login to offer "Sign in with Apple" beside Google. Leave it empty
# and the web login offers Google only (the iPhone app is unaffected either way).
# WHY: a browser cannot use the app's bundle id; Apple wants a separate "Services ID" for a website.
# In the Apple Developer portal: Identifiers > + > Services IDs, create one (e.g. ai.chordapp.web),
# tick Sign in with Apple, Configure: primary App ID = the Chord app, Domain = chordapp.ai,
# Return URL = https://chordapp.ai/api/v1/auth/apple/callback (must match exactly). Put the
# identifier here. It reuses APPLE_TEAM_ID / APPLE_KEY_ID / APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY, and needs WEB_ORIGIN
# (or SIGNUP_URL) to be an https address - Apple refuses http and localhost, so it stays off in dev.

# — 2026-10-01 — The read-time magics pass (ADR 0180) now preprocesses ahead of the open
# EMAIL_PREPROCESS_READTIME_ENABLED=true
# EMAIL_PREPROCESS_READTIME_BATCH=10
# WHEN: optional; the defaults are right. After a sync, and opportunistically on each thread open,
# the newest N not-yet-read-processed threads get the ADR 0180 pass run ahead of time, so opening
# one of them is instant instead of waiting on a live LLM call. Bounded to N at a time (not every
# synced thread) and self-advancing: once the top N are done, the same query surfaces the next N.
# Set EMAIL_PREPROCESS_READTIME_ENABLED=false to go back to purely on-open processing.

# — 2026-10-03 — Email images are fetched when the mail arrives, not when it is opened (ADR 0225)
# EMAIL_IMAGE_PRELOAD=true
# EMAIL_IMAGE_PRELOAD_BATCH=20
# EMAIL_IMAGE_PRELOAD_MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES=5242880
# EMAIL_IMAGE_PRELOAD_DAYS=30
# WHEN: optional; the defaults are right. After each mail sync the server fetches the remote images
# of new messages and stores them under DATA_DIR/email-images, so opening an email later tells the
# sender nothing. BATCH = messages per user per pass; MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES = stored bytes allowed per
# message (anything over loads when the mail is opened); DAYS = how long a stored image is kept and
# how far back mail is preloaded.
# WHY: without it the image is fetched when you open the mail, which tells the sender you read it
# and when. Costs bandwidth and disk for mail nobody reads; set EMAIL_IMAGE_PRELOAD=false to go
# back to fetching on open. Needs EMAIL_IMAGE_PROXY=true (the default).

# — 2026-10-04 — What is kept, and for how long (ADR 0226)
# RETENTION_ENABLED=true
# RETENTION_PROACTIVE_DAYS=30
# RETENTION_REJECTED_PROPOSAL_DAYS=15
# RETENTION_EXPIRED_PROPOSAL_DAYS=30
# RETENTION_TOKEN_USAGE_DAYS=365
# WHEN: optional; the defaults are right. Once a day the server deletes rows nothing reads any more:
# scheduled messages that have already been sent, suggestions that were rejected or that expired
# unanswered, and token-usage records older than a year. Each number is how many days that kind is
# kept; 0 keeps it forever. RETENTION_ENABLED=false turns the cleanup off.
# WHY: these tables were written and never deleted from. Suggestions you approved and the list of
# senders you unsubscribed from are never deleted. Logs are now limited by size instead (two files
# of 25 MB per log, fixed in code), and deploy.sh caps the system journal at 200 MB
# (JOURNAL_MAX_USE in scripts/deploy/deploy.env overrides).

# — 2026-10-08 — Family plan, annual billing, promo codes, App Store (ADR 0231)
# PRICE_INDIVIDUAL_USD=7.99
# PRICE_FAMILY_USD=24.99
# ANNUAL_FREE_MONTHS=1
# PRICE_INDIVIDUAL_YEARLY_USD=
# PRICE_FAMILY_YEARLY_USD=
# WHEN: optional; the defaults are the launch prices. These are the prices the APP SHOWS - the
# amount actually charged is the Stripe price / App Store product, so change both together.
# Annual = monthly x (12 - ANNUAL_FREE_MONTHS); the two *_YEARLY_USD figures override that.
# WHY: pricing was not settled when this shipped, and a price change should not need an app release.
# STRIPE_PRICE_ID_YEARLY=
# STRIPE_PRICE_ID_FAMILY=
# STRIPE_PRICE_ID_FAMILY_YEARLY=
# WHEN: when you create the other three recurring prices in Stripe (STRIPE_PRICE_ID is Individual
# monthly). Each is independent: an empty one is simply not offered. Also in the Stripe dashboard:
# save a Customer portal configuration (Settings > Billing > Customer portal) or "Manage" returns 400.
# WHY: one Stripe price per (plan, interval).
# FAMILY_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE=0
# GRANDFATHER_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE=20000000
# WHEN: optional. FAMILY = the one pool a 4-person family shares each month; 0 = 4 x MONTHLY_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE
# (see the 2026-11-05 entry at the bottom).
# GRANDFATHER = the monthly allowance for people here before billing went live.
# WHY: both are real costs - a family spends from one number, and a grandfathered user is free forever.
# REVENUECAT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# APPLE_PRODUCT_INDIVIDUAL_MONTHLY=chord.individual.monthly
# APPLE_PRODUCT_INDIVIDUAL_YEARLY=chord.individual.yearly
# APPLE_PRODUCT_FAMILY_MONTHLY=chord.family.monthly
# APPLE_PRODUCT_FAMILY_YEARLY=chord.family.yearly
# WHEN: when you set up the App Store subscriptions. In RevenueCat add a webhook to
# https://<your-host>/api/v1/webhooks/revenuecat with this value as its Authorization header; the
# product ids must match what you create in App Store Connect (the defaults above are what the app
# expects unless you set these). Empty secret = the webhook 404s and the app offers no App Store purchase.
# WHY: RevenueCat validates receipts and Apple's notifications and tells us one thing: who is paid until when.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_REVENUECAT_IOS_KEY=
# WHEN: set in the shell / eas.json for an iOS BUILD (it is the public SDK key, not a secret). The App
# Store purchase needs a new native build - the SDK is a native module and does not arrive by OTA update.
# WHY: without it the iOS app falls back to the web checkout, as before.
# One-off commands (run on the server with the app's environment):
#   npm run promo:codes --workspace=server -- generate --count 20 --note "friends"
#   npm run grandfather:users --workspace=server -- --before 2026-11-01          (dry run)
#   npm run grandfather:users --workspace=server -- --before 2026-11-01 --apply   (do it, once, just
#                                                                            before BILLING_ENABLED=true)

# — 2026-10-08 — Outlook calendars, two-way (ADR 0228)
# OUTLOOK_CALENDAR_ENABLED=false
# WHEN: set to true once the Microsoft app registration (the MS_CLIENT_ID one) lists the
# Calendars.ReadWrite delegated permission. From then on, connecting Outlook also asks for
# calendar access, and that mailbox's calendars sync both ways like Google's. A mailbox
# connected earlier stays mail-only until the user adds Outlook again in Settings.
# WHY: off by default because it has not yet been run against a live Microsoft mailbox, and
# because asking for a permission the app registration does not list can fail the whole
# Outlook connect, mail included. Needs MS_CLIENT_ID / MS_CLIENT_SECRET; no other key.

# — 2026-10-09 — Connect OpenRouter as your own key (ADR 0235)
# OPENROUTER_DEFAULT_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6
# WHEN: only matters if the app's own model has no OpenRouter spelling (LLM_PROVIDER=mock, or
# a system default on some other OpenAI-compatible endpoint). Otherwise a new connection starts
# on the model the app already runs on (gpt-5.4-mini -> openai/gpt-5.4-mini) with no setting.
# WHY: the "Connect OpenRouter" button needs a model to validate the new key against. The flow
# also needs WEB_ORIGIN (the public https origin) because OpenRouter redirects the browser
# back to <WEB_ORIGIN>/api/v1/openrouter/callback/<state>; without it the button answers 503.

# — 2026-10-10 — The BYOK base plan, in both stores (ADR 0238)
# PRICE_BYOK_USD=2.99
# APPLE_PRODUCT_BYOK_MONTHLY=chord.byok.monthly
# WHEN: PRICE_BYOK_USD only if the base plan's Stripe price is not $2.99 (it is what the app SHOWS; the
# charge is the Stripe price / App Store product). APPLE_PRODUCT_BYOK_MONTHLY only if you named the App
# Store subscription something other than the default. The fee itself switches on when
# STRIPE_PRICE_ID_BYOK (above) is set to the base plan's Stripe price id.
# WHY: a bring-your-own-key user still costs hosting and messaging; this is the fixed part of that cost.
# Create the same product in App Store Connect (monthly, in the same subscription group as the other four,
# ranked lowest) so the iOS app can sell it without sending anyone to a web checkout.

# — 2026-10-12 — Several suggestions from one email (ADR 0243)
# EMAIL_MAGICS_MAX_ITEMS=4
# WHEN: only to change how many suggestion cards one email may produce. Each event, each to-do and
# each list counts as one card. Set 1 to go back to one suggestion per email.
# WHY: a long email (a school bulletin with a form, a date and things to bring) used to yield one
# arbitrary card and lose the rest for good, since an email is only read for suggestions once.

# — 2026-10-15 — Receipts kept after their email is deleted (ADR 0249)
# RETENTION_RECEIPT_DAYS=1095
# WHEN: only to keep saved receipts for more or less than three years (counted from the email's
# date). 0 keeps them forever. Deleting the account removes them at once either way.
# WHY: totals and repeating charges no longer drop when you trash a receipt email, so the copy
# needs its own end date. Three years lets a yearly charge show up as repeating.

# — 2026-10-15 — BYOK is free (ADR 0251)
# STRIPE_PRICE_ID_BYOK=
# WHEN: leave it EMPTY (prod is). No new variable — an empty base price is what makes a
# bring-your-own-key user free and never asked for a base plan. Set it only to charge BYOK again.
# WHY: the $2.99 base fee is shelved; what a BYOK user with WhatsApp pays waits on measured usage.

# — 2026-10-15 — Where people message Chord on WhatsApp (ADR 0253)
# WHATSAPP_PUBLIC_NUMBER=+15555550123
# WHEN: to show an "Open WhatsApp" entry in Settings and on the sign-in page. E.164, with the +.
# Change it here when the WhatsApp business number changes - the app reads it at runtime, so that is
# a redeploy, not an app release. Empty hides the entry. (The Meta phone-number id and token that
# let Chord SEND from the new number are separate - META_WHATSAPP_*.)
# WHY: until now nothing in the app said Chord could be reached on WhatsApp, or where.

# — 2026-10-16 — Sessions end on the server (CASA)
# SESSION_IDLE_DAYS=30
# SESSION_MAX_DAYS=90
# WHEN: only to change how long a sign-in lasts. A session unused for SESSION_IDLE_DAYS, or older
# than SESSION_MAX_DAYS however busy, is refused (401) and deleted, so the person signs in again.
# 0 turns that limit off. The web cookie lasts SESSION_MAX_DAYS too.
# WHY: a session token used to work until sign-out, forever on a phone. Google's CASA security
# assessment asks for session expiry as well as working sign-out.

# — 2026-10-19 — The next email is checked while you read this one
# EMAIL_PREFETCH_READTIME_ENABLED=true
# WHEN: only to turn it off (false) if the extra model calls are not wanted.
# WHY: opening an email waited up to EMAIL_READ_TIME_WAIT_MS for its one-time check. When the
# reader pre-loads the next/previous email it now starts that email's check too, so opening it is
# instant. Same mail rule as the warm-up (unread or recent, not bulk/coupons/newsletters); costs a
# model call for a neighbour you never open.

# — 2026-10-19 — Meaning-based search (ADR 0276)
# EMBEDDINGS_ENABLED=false
# EMBEDDINGS_BASE_URL=
# EMBEDDINGS_API_KEY=
# EMBEDDINGS_MODEL=openai/text-embedding-3-small
# EMBEDDINGS_DIMS=512
# EMBEDDINGS_BYOK_FALLBACK=chord
# EMBED_PASS_MAX_PER_TICK=200
# EMBED_SWEEP_BATCH=10
# EMBED_CACHE_USERS=50
# SEARCH_SEMANTIC_MIN_SIM=0.3
# WHEN: set EMBEDDINGS_ENABLED=true to let chat search match on meaning ("the school trip form"
# finds "Field trip permission slip"). Nothing else is needed when LLM_PROVIDER=openai: the base URL
# and key default to OPENAI_BASE_URL / OPENAI_API_KEY (prod: OpenRouter). Set EMBEDDINGS_BASE_URL /
# EMBEDDINGS_API_KEY only when Chord's chat model is not OpenAI-compatible (e.g. Anthropic), or to
# embed on a different account. On a direct OpenAI key use EMBEDDINGS_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small
# (no "openai/" prefix). EMBEDDINGS_BYOK_FALLBACK=none leaves users with their own Anthropic key on
# keyword search instead of embedding their mail on Chord's key.
# WHY: keyword search cannot find an email that says the same thing in other words. Backfill costs
# about $0.16 per user once (ADR 0257 row).

# — 2026-10-20 — AI only after the user agrees (ADR 0283)
# AI_CONSENT_REQUIRED=true
# WHEN: nothing to add; it is on by default. Set false only on a dev box where you do not want to
# click through the consent screen.
# WHY: Apple guideline 5.1.2(i) needs explicit permission before personal data goes to a
# third-party AI. With it on, every user's AI (bot replies, email suggestions, briefing, meaning
# search) waits until they press Allow in the app or reply AGREE in chat. Existing users are asked
# too, so their AI pauses until they answer.

# — 2026-10-20 — Chord says what it remembers (ADR 0281)
# MEMORY_CLEANUP_ENABLED=true
# MEMORY_CLEANUP_HOURS=24
# MEMORY_PROPOSED_TTL_DAYS=30
# MEMORY_CLEANUP_GROUPS_PER_RUN=10
# WHEN: nothing to add; the defaults are on. Set MEMORY_CLEANUP_ENABLED=false to stop the pass.
# WHY: once a day the pass removes suggested family facts that repeat a saved one, keeps only the
# newest suggestion per fact, drops suggestions nobody answered for MEMORY_PROPOSED_TTL_DAYS, and
# asks the model which saved facts say the same thing (at most MEMORY_CLEANUP_GROUPS_PER_RUN chats a
# run, only chats whose facts changed). The model only picks duplicates; it never writes a value.

# — 2026-10-22 — Only Chord's own websites can call the API from a browser (ADR 0269 addendum)
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=
# WHEN: only if a browser app on ANOTHER origin must call this server (comma-separated, e.g. https://staging.chordapp.ai).
# WHY: the server used to reflect any Origin with credentials, which the CASA scan failed (6.3.1). chordapp.ai,
# www.chordapp.ai, WEB_ORIGIN and SIGNUP_URL are always allowed; in development any localhost/LAN address is too.

# — 2026-10-22 — Files go into a bot turn's prompt only when relevant (ADR 0288)
# RETRIEVAL_SEMANTIC_MIN_SIM=0.35
# WHEN: nothing to add. Only matters with EMBEDDINGS_ENABLED=true; raise it if unrelated files still
# reach the prompt, lower it if a file the question is plainly about is left out.
# WHY: a file with a vector goes into the prompt only at this similarity or above; without one it needs
# a filename word, or two (and half) of a message's words as whole words. A restaurant answer was footed
# "based on" four contracts because one word ("Friday") was enough before.

# — 2026-10-23 — Sign in with Microsoft (ADR 0293)
# MICROSOFT_SIGN_IN_ENABLED=false
# WHEN: to offer a third login for people whose mail is Outlook/Hotmail/Microsoft 365. Needs MS_CLIENT_ID
# and MS_CLIENT_SECRET (the Outlook mailbox app, ADR 0080) and AUTH_MODE=google. Before turning it on, add the
# xms_edov optional ID-token claim in Entra (Token configuration) and check the redirect URIs include
# <web origin>/oauthredirect and chord://oauthredirect.
# WHY: signing in with Microsoft creates (or finds) the account AND connects the Outlook mailbox in one consent.
# Without xms_edov a work/school account never joins an existing account by email; it gets its own.

# — 2026-10-23 — Deleted phone-calendar rows expire (ADR 0057 addendum, ADR 0226)
# RETENTION_DELETED_DEVICE_EVENT_DAYS=30
# WHEN: nothing to add; 0 keeps them forever.
# WHY: the phone calendar sync marks a row deleted when the phone no longer has the event and never
# reads it again. A subscribed iOS calendar re-issues its event ids on every refresh, which once left
# 71,525 such rows for one user. The daily clean-up now removes them 30 days after deletion. Past
# events that were not deleted are kept.

# — 2026-10-26 — Unused settings removed
# WHEN: delete these from your .env / .env.local / scripts/deploy/.env.prod if they are still there:
#   GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI, EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI_WEB, EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI_NATIVE,
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_ANDROID, PROACTIVE_RATE_MINUTES, CREDITS_PER_BUNDLE, DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN,
#   SLACK_BOT_TOKEN, SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET.
# WHY: no code reads them. The sign-in redirect comes from the client on each request, Android signs in
# with the web client (ADR 0081), and the rest were renamed or retired (ADR 0154, 0194, 2026-10-13).

# — 2026-10-27 — Drafts copied to the mailbox's own Drafts folder (ADR 0303)
# WHEN: nothing to add; the default is 30. Set 0 to keep drafts in Chord only.
# WHY: a draft being written in Chord is copied to Gmail / Outlook / Yahoo Drafts at most this often
# (seconds), and removed there once sent or discarded.
EMAIL_PROVIDER_DRAFT_SECONDS=30

# — 2026-11-02 — A phone calendar the phone stopped sending is forgotten (ADR 0057 addendum, ADR 0226)
# RETENTION_DEVICE_CALENDAR_DAYS=30
# WHEN: nothing to add; 0 keeps them forever.
# WHY: a phone calendar unticked from Mirror, removed from the phone, or on a phone no longer used kept
# its Settings row and its mirrored events forever. The daily clean-up now removes both once no phone has
# sent that calendar for this many days. Time, not "missing from the last sync", because a second phone
# sends only its own calendars.

# — 2026-11-05 — A chat turn is sent the tools it needs, not all of them (ADR 0322)
# BOT_TOOL_SUBSET=true
# WHEN: nothing to add; set false only to go back to sending every tool on every turn.
# WHY: the full catalogue of ~120 tools was ~35k tokens of every round trip of every chat turn. A turn
# now gets a core set plus the topic groups its conversation names, and can load the rest itself.

# — 2026-11-05 — The family pool follows the individual allowance (ADR 0231 addendum)
# FAMILY_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE=0
# WHEN: remove FAMILY_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE from your .env if you only set it to 4 x the individual figure.
# Set it above 0 only to give families a pool that is NOT 4 x MONTHLY_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE.
# WHY: the default is now 0 = FAMILY_SEATS (4) x MONTHLY_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE, so raising the individual
# allowance raises the family's with it. It used to be a fixed 20M whatever the individual value was.

# — 2026-11-05 — A free allowance is tokens, not days (ADR 0325)
# FREE_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE=10000000
# WHEN: remove TRIAL_DAYS from your .env (no longer read). Add this only to give a figure other than
# 10M; 0 turns the free allowance off. Ignored while BILLING_ENABLED=false.
# WHY: the 30-day trial is replaced by a one-time free allowance per account, counted from sign-up with
# no time limit. Accounts past their old trial with less than this used get AI back.

# — 2026-11-10 — A group event invites the family (ADR 0328)
# FAMILY_EVENT_INVITES=false
# WHEN: nothing to add for now. Turn it on only after a two-account test against a live Google and a
# live Outlook calendar.
# WHY: a shared-group event wrote a silent copy onto each member's calendar, so nobody was told when
# it moved and nobody could answer. On, the organizer's event invites the members (from their Google or
# Outlook calendar, else as a calendar invitation mailed from their mailbox); a member Chord cannot
# invite still gets a copy.

# — 2026-11-10 — An allowance is a dollar budget, shown in real tokens (ADR 0332)
# ALLOWANCE_USD_PER_MILLION_TOKENS=0.4
# WHEN: nothing to add unless you want a different price. Every token allowance (free, monthly,
# family, grandfathered) is a dollar budget of its tokens x this per million: 10M = $4 at 0.4.
# WHY: spending is now measured in the dollars OpenRouter reports for each call. Cached prompt
# tokens are cheap, so users ran out of tokens on a small bill; costly calls could overspend.
# 0 = count plain tokens (the old rule).
# — 2026-11-11 — Allowance budgets in dollars (ADR 0332)
# MONTHLY_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE=20000000  GRANDFATHER_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE=20000000  FREE_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE=12500000
# WHEN: set MONTHLY_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE and GRANDFATHER_TOKEN_ALLOWANCE to 20000000 in your .env (or
# remove them to take the defaults). FREE has no .env line unless you set one.
# WHY: at ALLOWANCE_USD_PER_MILLION_TOKENS=0.4, a paid month is $8 of model spend (20M) and the
# one-time free allowance $5 (12.5M); both were 10M = $4.

# — 2026-11-12 — Chord as a connector in Claude and ChatGPT (ADR 0335)
# MCP_CONNECTOR_ENABLED=false
# WHEN: nothing to add until you want the connector live. On, WEB_ORIGIN must be the public https
# origin (it is the OAuth issuer and the MCP URL). See docs/ops/mcp-connector.md.
# WHY: Claude / ChatGPT can connect to Chord (remote MCP + OAuth) to read mail, to-dos and the
# calendar and leave suggestions in Action Items. Off = every connector route answers 404.

# — 2026-11-17 — App review sign-in (ADR 0345)
# REVIEW_SIGN_IN_EMAIL=
# REVIEW_SIGN_IN_PASSWORD=
# WHEN: only on prod, while a store review needs it. Email = the demo Google account's address
# (sign in to Chord with that Google account once first and connect its mail); password = 20+
# random characters (a secret, kept in Secret Manager). Both empty = off, and the app shows no link.
# WHY: Google Play's reviewer could not get past Google's "verify it's you" on a new device. This
# email + password opens that one account and nothing else.

# — 2026-11-23 — Re-engage feature tips (ADR 0351)
# REENGAGE_ENABLED=true
# REENGAGE_IDLE_DAYS=2
# REENGAGE_MAX_IDLE_DAYS=21
# REENGAGE_MIN_GAP_DAYS=7
# REENGAGE_SWEEP_MINUTES=30
# REENGAGE_BATCH=50
# REENGAGE_START_HOUR=10
# REENGAGE_END_HOUR=19
# WHEN: nothing to add; on by default (needs PUSH_ENABLED). Set false to stop all tips.
# WHY: someone who has not opened the app for IDLE_DAYS gets ONE push naming a feature they have
# not used yet ("Long email? Tap tldr"), between START_HOUR and END_HOUR in their own zone, at most
# one per absence and never closer than MIN_GAP_DAYS. Each tip ends "Reply stop sending me tips
in chat to stop"; someone with notifications off gets nothing.
